On 7/6/2013 5:53 PM, ais523 wrote:
> So, a long time ago (many months), there was a (small) concerted effort
> to get NetHack 4 running on Windows, which mostly failed; someone
> succeeded, but not in a way that anyone else would reasonably be able to
> replicate.
>
> I've been working on the problem again, and actually succeeded! However,
> it's still very early stages: the installation is rather complex (see
> file README.Win32), and there are known (and somewhat major) performance
> problems. Getting it working on Windows was much easier this time due to
> two libraries that didn't exist at the last attempt:
>
> - The build works using a newly rewritten version of aimake, aimake2.
>    I designed aimake2 with portability in mind from the start, and as a
>    result, it works just fine on Windows. Unfortunately, it is very slow,
>    to the extent that it is not really usable if you have better options.
>
> - The rendering is abstracted through a new library, libuncursed. I wrote
>    libuncursed for UNIX/Linux in order to solve several problems with
>    curses (and it's currently powering nethack4.org), but because it
>    abstracts rendering down to a few primitives, I was able to make a
>    Windows version as well with only an extra 2 days of effort. As such,
>    NetHack 4 on Windows uses the Windows low-level console API for
>    rendering. However, just like aimake2, it is /also/ very slow, meaning
>    that a keypress can leave the screen blank for an appreciable fraction
>    of a second; it's worse than playing on NAO from Europe, which is
>    pretty bad lag for a local executable.
>
> I also had to make some reasonably dubious changes (replacing 64-bit
> variables with 32-bit variables, that sort of thing) in the code just to
> get it to compile, and doing so is likely to have introduced bugs
> (localised to the "windows" branch; I have a separate branch for the
> more stable code on the Linux version). So be prepared for bugs, even
> stupid ones, that don't exist on other versions.
>
> The (developer preview) Windows version of NetHack 4 can be downloaded
> at <https://gitorious.org/nitrohack/ais523/archive-tarball/windows>. Or
> if you have a working installation of git, you can clone branch
> "windows" of the repository (<git://gitorious.org/nitrohack/ais523.git>).
>
> Let me know what you think. Bug reports, and particularly patches, are
> welcome: NetHack 4's bug tracker is at <http://trac.nethack4.org/>
> (which can be used for all bugs, not just Windows-related bugs).
>
> Finally, note that there is no tiles version yet; everyone's still stuck
> with ASCII, on every platform. That's the obvious next step, though (and
> it would hopefully go some way towards fixing the performance problems,
> too).
>

